-- §2  The compositions as balls into boxes: every splice site, and the template it lands in.
# One row per (parent, child) edge of the compose DAG, with how many times the parent splices the
# child. This is the labelled reading of the function *splice site -> child*, handed over whole.
#
# The other three readings are taken in Rust
#   The fibre sizes (how many sites land in each template), the kernel (which parents compose the
#   same set of children) and the shape (the profile of fibre sizes) are each one `GROUP BY` away
#   from here. Writing them as three more templates would be the tree describing itself in its own
#   vocabulary, so `examples/combinatorics/main.rs` derives them from the edge list by a route that
#   shares no SQL with rank/.
#
# The zeros come from the directory
#   A template nothing composes has no edge, so it cannot appear in any fibre this relation
#   carries: `GROUP BY` enumerates the image. The program drives from `assets/sqlc/` on disk to see
#   them, the same move the `unreached` law in `examples/observations/main.rs` makes.
-- rank/compose_edges.sqlc, the edge list as it stands.
SELECT e.parent AS "parent!", e.child AS "child!", e.splices::bigint AS "splices!"
FROM (
    :compose(rank/compose_edges.sqlc)
) e
ORDER BY e.parent, e.child
